                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p22-q73-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Factor part (a) as a difference of squares; the two factors have product 1. Use ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "2=\\sqrt4",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "By part (a),\n$$[\\sqrt2(1+\\sqrt3)]^2-(2+\\sqrt3)^2=1.$$\nFactor the difference of squares:\n$$[\\sqrt2(1+\\sqrt3)-(2+\\sqrt3)][\\sqrt2(1+\\sqrt3)+(2+\\sqrt3)]=1.$$\nThus\n$$(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6-2-\\sqrt3)(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6+2+\\sqrt3)=1.$$\nThe second factor is positive, so division gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6+2+\\sqrt3}&=\\sqrt2+\\sqrt6-2-\\sqrt3\\\\\\frac1{\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt4+\\sqrt6}&=\\sqrt2-\\sqrt3-\\sqrt4+\\sqrt6\\end{align*}$$\nHere $2=\\sqrt4$, as required.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 74
